      "content": "Religious conservatives generally argue that life is sacred and inviolable from conception, viewing physician-assisted dying as a violation of the \"right to life\" and a slippery slope toward abortion and euthanasia. In contrast, religious liberals often contend that suffering is a legitimate concern within God's will and that the duty of compassion may override the absolute prohibition against killing when it relieves intractable agony. These opposing moral frameworks often shape how conservative groups advocate for strict legislative restrictions while liberal groups push for minority exemptions or specific provisions for the terminally ill. Ultimately, the debate centers on whether the preservation of biological life takes absolute precedence or if the relief of unbearable suffering is a sufficient moral justification for intervention.",
